_:genid1378 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/tvbo/struct> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "GraphRepresentation" .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "How a Network's connectivity is represented when evaluating coupling. This is a backend-independent performance contract, not a numerical one: both representations compute the same coupling, but a sparse representation sums over the existing edges (cost scales with the number of edges) while a dense representation operates on the full adjacency (cost scales with nodes-squared). A backend maps the choice onto its own primitives (edge gather / segment-sum vs matrix product). Orthogonal to whether the coupling reads *delayed* states — that is decided per coupling by its conduction delay — so the two dimensions together yield the four backend graph kinds: dense/sparse × instantaneous/delayed." .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/tvbo> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <https://w3id.org/linkml/permissible_values> <https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation#auto> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <https://w3id.org/linkml/permissible_values> <https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation#dense> .
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation> <https://w3id.org/linkml/permissible_values> <https://w3id.org/tvbo/GraphRepresentation#sparse> .
